The literature is very clear on this — and your question actually raises three distinct problems
that deserve to be separated properly.
 
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
THE THREE PROBLEMS OF P/F IN YOUR TOOL
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 
PROBLEM 1 — P/F is not independent of FiO₂ (your question about FiO₂ 100%)
 
The P/F ratio varies according to the FiO₂ used to calculate it: with the same intrapulmonary
shunt, increasing FiO₂ decreases P/F — which can classify a patient as more severe ARDS
even though their gas exchange is unchanged. A study on COVID ARDS patients showed that
FiO₂ = 1.0 gave the best agreement with classification based on actual shunt calculated
by Swan-Ganz oximetry. In other words: those who say 'calculate at FiO₂ 100% to remove shunt effects' have solid physiological grounds — but this is not what Berlin does in practice. P/F varies according to the degree of shunt present: increasing FiO₂ raises P/F if shunt is small, but lowers it if shunt is large. P/F reflects neither oxygen content of blood nor tissue O₂ delivery.
 
Consequence for your tool: P/F is contextually dependent on the FiO₂ entered by the user. This is a real limitation — but it is also the limitation of all existing tools, including Berlin. You document it, you don't hide it.
 
PROBLEM 2 — SpO₂ vs PaO₂: are they interchangeable?
 
SpO₂/FiO₂ is determined primarily by FiO₂ settings rather than by the measured SpO₂ — increasing FiO₂ lowers the SpO₂/FiO₂ ratio despite unchanged gas exchange. The correlation of SpO₂ to FiO₂ changes is weak (r = 0.42), versus r = 0.88 for PaO₂. The two ratios must not be used interchangeably. But the important nuance: the SpO₂/FiO₂ ratio with thresholds of 146, 236, and 296 corresponds to the P/F thresholds of 100, 200, and 300 — with AUC ROC of 0.98, 0.95 and 0.95 respectively for ARDS diagnosis.
 
What this means concretely: SpO₂/FiO₂ classifies ARDS well in terms of mortality, but is not calculated like P/F. They are two different indices with different thresholds.
 
PROBLEM 3 — Is arterial blood gas strictly required?
 
PaO₂/FiO₂ remains the gold standard for diagnosis of acute respiratory failure. SpO₂/FiO₂ is a validated substitute mainly in resource-limited settings or for continuous monitoring — with good diagnostic performance when SpO₂ is between 80 and 97%.  In your context (medical ARDS at admission in the ICU at Marie Lannelongue): an arterial blood gas will be quasi-systematically available at admission. SpO₂ as fallback is legitimate but must be clearly signaled as an estimate.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
