Claude
Yes, exactly — and this is an important physiological nuance.
 
DP = Pplat − PEEP is the measured definition in clinical practice, from the pressures
displayed by the ventilator on a passive patient during inspiratory occlusion.
 
DP = VT / CRS is the physical definition, which follows directly from the law of elasticity
applied to the respiratory system: driving pressure is the ratio of tidal volume to respiratory system compliance (compliance = ΔV/ΔP, so ΔP = ΔV/C).
 
The two are algebraically equivalent — two ways of expressing the same reality. This is precisely why Amato et al. in their NEJM 2015 paper proposed driving pressure as the best proxy for pulmonary stress: because DP = VT / CRS implicitly normalises VT by the functional lung size (represented by CRS). A VT of 6 mL/kg PBW can give a very high DP if CRS is collapsed (baby lung in severe ARDS) — this is the whole message of Amato.
 
For your tool and manuscript, the formula Pplat − PEEP is the right one to implement because it's what's available at the bedside without additional calculation. The VT/CRS formulation is useful in the Discussion to explain why DP is superior to VT/PBW alone as a mortality predictor — this is exactly the argument of PROSEVA and Amato.
 
You could also mention this equivalence — it shows the pathophysiological depth of your specification.
